r notes are a  distraction  trying to fill you with irrelevant  information the korean book was  particularly bad  the the russian one was was okay they uh  you know there was a little humor in  some of their dialogues uh it progresses  quite quickly uh what i don't like about  asimil is they don't have a vocabulary  list for each  lesson so you're forced to read the  translation which is less efficient  because i got to look for the words that  i don't understand i don't want the  whole thing translated i don't want  their explanations i just want the sound  and the text but asimil is another  beginner one  you know i bought a berlitz book in  russian don't forget i had all of this  before we had link in russia what i  would do now  uh  you know  i think i would still recommend to some  people that even with link  that many of them may want to have a  little book you can get yourself a  little grammar i mean there are little  grammar books like this  or this series was particularly good it  was recommended  by someone on my who commented on my  blog it's the dover  essential grammar books again i always  recommend getting the smallest possible  grammar book you can find and reading  through it from time to time  um  with russian  uh you know i i uh let's see what else  did i buy i bought a book like this  called 500 rational verbs i never use it  i keep it by my toilet occasionally when  i sit there i leave through it i don't  really remember much of what i leave  through it's essentially a useless book  for me  uh i bought this for russian called  beginners russian reader but i found  reading this stuff boring  and  instead when i found this book here  which is the kreutzer sonata  it's a bilingual book french and english  and uh  you know  the sooner you get on to at least with  me the sooner i find i'm able to access  authentic content i'm so much more  motivated to learn it and i was able to  get the kreuzer sonata  book with the french which to me is not  as nice as having the word list but at  least it gives you the meaning so you  can work your way through it once we had  link i was able to find kreutzer sonata  at gutenberg.org or somewhere i loaded  it into link and from then on i was just  on link  i used  link really with with we have some of  our beginning material that link as soon  as we had link up and running for  russian i didn't need any of the books  and  what i and i'll tell you why that is  like even when i was doing german and  italian 20 years ago i would scour the  bookstores here in vancouver looking for  readers  which had text  and a word list that's all i wanted  because i know it's inefficient to look  up in a dictionary because any word that  you see in a dictionary or in a list  you're going to forget so i would rather  have the word list associated with the  text that i'm reading  and then i have a chance of remembering  and plus it's there right away but the  word lists aren't great because very  often the words you need aren't on the  word list  and half the words on the word list you  already know so it's not  that efficient  and rarely what i was telling before  would i be so spoiled as to have the  sound as well as the text  so to my mind what we do now at link is  just an unbelievable service for free  you can go to the library and there you  have in 10 languages content with  guaranteed sound and text  and all the other advantages you can  save you're connected to a to an online  dictionary you can save words to a  database uh you know we start to  indicate how many unknown words or  anyway i won't go into talking about  link  so in all of that then what am i saying  oh i wanted to say one more thing  insofar as the beginner the beginning  period the first three months  and first three months is kind of where  you get over the unfamiliarity you're  not going to learn anything to keep  you're not going to learn how to say  hello my name is so-and-so and then for  forever never be able to 